 | 2026-08-22 | experiment_claim_audit | experiment_6478_identifiable_held_exact_energy_selection.json | CLAIM_OVERSTATED | ACCEPTED | 2026-08-23 outer-loop. Finding is correct. The verdict says it itself: `complete_positive: ... exact backend remains the oracle`, with `verifier_is_oracle: true` and `held_exact_energy_selection_ready_score: 1.0`. A verifier that IS the oracle cannot produce a research win, so this is `execution_grounded`, not a selection result. The measurement stands; the framing does not. NOT headline- or capstone-eligible, and not evidence that energy selection beats a baseline. No verdict_class was declared, which is how it escaped the existing positive-vs-oracle cross-check. A lint for that gap was built and REMOVED the same day: it fired on 221 historical artifacts, because declaring the class is forward-only from 2026-08-21 and this artifact carries no date field to separate it from them. Prevention for NEW artifacts is the planner emitting verdict_class, after which positive + verifier_is_oracle is already CRITICAL. Artifact left unedited per never-prune. |
 | 2026-08-22 | experiment_claim_audit | experiment_6497_factor_pool_support_stress.json | CLAIM_OVERSTATED | ACCEPTED | 2026-08-23 outer-loop. Same shape as exp6478 and same disposition. `complete_positive:` with `verifier_is_oracle: true`, no verdict_class, and both headline numbers at exactly 1.0 (`support_preserved_score`, `support_stress_complete_score`). Perfect scores from the oracle that defines correctness are a tautology, not a stress-test pass. Reads honestly as "the exact evaluator agreed with itself under load"; it is not evidence the pool preserves support against an independent standard. NOT headline-eligible. See the exp6478 note for why no lint was added for this shape. |
 | 2026-08-23 | experiment_claim_audit | experiment_6521_transactional_refinement_conflict_memory.json | CLAIM_OVERSTATED | ACCEPTED | 2026-08-23 outer-loop. Finding is correct and the artifact is the honest version of the same problem: it declares `verdict_class: circular_positive`, so the circularity already travels with the claim and no lint fires. What is overstated is READINESS — `unsafe_admission_count: 0` and `unsafe_use_count: 0` were measured by the exact-replay verifier that also gates acceptance, so no independent oracle could have disagreed. Audit recommendation ADD_MISSING_CONTROL is right and is future work, not a correction to this artifact. Usable as a lifecycle/recovery result; NOT usable as a safety claim until an oracle-distinct control exists. |
-| 2026-08-23 | experiment_claim_audit | experiment_6544_external_structural_headroom.json | CLAIM_OVERSTATED | OPEN | |
-| 2026-08-23 | experiment_claim_audit | experiment_6546_smt_cost_guard_sota.json | CLAIM_OVERSTATED | OPEN | |
+| 2026-08-23 | experiment_claim_audit | experiment_6544_external_structural_headroom.json | CLAIM_OVERSTATED | ACCEPTED | 2026-08-23 outer-loop. Finding is correct. The measurement is real and the arithmetic holds (`held_total_charged_work_by_arm`: native 2532, random 2532, analytical 1986), but the win is structural rather than earned. The candidate set always contains the current target prefix, analytical ordering selects the largest prefix first, and evaluation stops on reaching the full target state — so chronological and random ordering cannot seriously compete by construction. `no_headroom_pair_count: 0` across 54 pairs is the tell: a comparison where no pair lacks headroom is a comparison whose baselines were never a threat. Note this is NOT the exp6478/exp6497 circular-oracle shape; `verifier_is_oracle: false` is correctly declared and the verifier genuinely is oracle-distinct. The defect is a strawman baseline, which no existing lint detects. Usable as evidence that analytical ordering beats chronological and random; NOT evidence of added analytical value, and NOT headline-eligible. Audit recommendation ADD_MISSING_CONTROL is right: the missing arm is a deterministic deepest-first / current-turn-prefix-first baseline under identical overhead accounting. That is future work, not a correction to this artifact, which is left unedited per never-prune. |
+| 2026-08-23 | experiment_claim_audit | experiment_6546_smt_cost_guard_sota.json | CLAIM_OVERSTATED | ACCEPTED | 2026-08-23 outer-loop. Finding is correct, and it is the same shape as exp6544 on the same day: the winning arm is favoured by the accounting rather than by being better. Guarded dispatch shows 3029 charged tokens against 4636 unguarded, but routed exact-Z3 calls incur no model-token charge at all, so routing work to Z3 reduces the measured cost by definition; and Z3 replacement cannot score worse than the model on exact completion under the stated evaluation, so non-inferiority is guaranteed rather than observed. Either a token OR a time saving sufficed, which weakens the bar further. Scale is small: `conflict_threshold: 1` with only `guarded_direct_tool_rows: 2`. Usable as an engineering result that routing to an exact solver is cheaper under this charging model; NOT evidence the GUARD adds value, since the cheapest serious control — always dispatch to Z3 — was never run, and an always-Z3 arm tying or winning would refute the guard's whole premise. ADD_MISSING_CONTROL accepted as future work. Artifact left unedited per never-prune. PROCESS NOTE worth keeping: this reasoning was NOT in the current report, which the conductor overwrites at each milestone-close. It was recovered from commit 1d35738746. A ledger row therefore points at evidence that survives only because the conductor commits the report — recoverable, but not by design. |
